  content: `
<h2>The real problem is not data, it is wasted data</h2>
<p>If you study from a phone in Nigeria, you already know the feeling. You buy a data bundle, open one video, and the bundle starts to disappear. Then a few apps update in the background, a group chat downloads photos, and by the middle of the week you are out.</p>
<p>The answer is not to stop studying on your phone. It is to make sure that every megabyte you spend goes to something that moves your marks. A student who spends a small amount of data well can learn more than a student who spends a large amount carelessly.</p>
<p>This guide gives you a system with three parts: <strong>stop the leaks</strong>, <strong>plan your downloads</strong>, and <strong>study offline</strong>. None of it requires special tools. It requires a little discipline and a weekly routine.</p>
[[callout key|The idea in one line|Connect briefly, download what you need, then disconnect and study. Learning happens offline. Data is only the delivery truck.]]

<h2>Step 1: Find where your data is going</h2>
<p>You cannot fix what you do not measure. Most phones show how much data each app has used. Look in your phone settings under network, data usage or mobile data. Check the list for the past week or month.</p>
<p>Look at the top five apps. For many students, social media, video apps and app stores are at the top, not study resources. This is a good sign, not a bad one, because it means there is a lot of room to save without hurting your learning.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Social media and chat apps:</strong> these download photos, videos and status updates automatically. Turn off auto-download of media on mobile data.</li>
<li><strong>Video apps:</strong> auto-play and high quality settings use data fast. Switch auto-play off and lower the quality.</li>
<li><strong>App stores and system updates:</strong> set updates to happen only on Wi-Fi, or turn off automatic updates.</li>
<li><strong>Cloud backups and photo sync:</strong> pause these on mobile data.</li>
</ul>
<p>Settings have different names on different phones, but they are all in the same few places: the app's own settings, the phone's data usage or data saver menu, and the app store settings.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Turn on the leak-stoppers</h2>
<p>Spend ten minutes now to save data for the whole term. Use this list.</p>
[[checklist Turn on Data Saver or Low Data Mode in your phone settings ; Restrict background data for apps you do not need ; Switch off auto-play of videos in social media apps ; Turn off automatic media download on mobile data in chat apps ; Set app updates to Wi-Fi only ; Pause cloud backup and photo sync on mobile data ; Turn off mobile data when you are not using it]]
<p>The last item is the most powerful. If you only turn mobile data on when you need it, apps cannot spend data without your knowledge. Many students keep data on all day and wonder where it went.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Choose study resources that cost little data</h2>
<p>Not all study materials use the same amount of data. Text is light. Images cost more. Audio is heavier. Video is the heaviest. Use that order when you plan.</p>
[[bars Typical data use, lightest to heaviest (relative idea, not exact)|Text page=1 ; Image-heavy page=5 ; Audio lesson=20 ; Video lesson=100|units]]
<p>The numbers above are an illustration of relative size, not exact figures, because real file sizes depend on length, quality and format. The point is simple: a page of text and past questions costs a fraction of a video of the same topic.</p>
<p>That does not mean you should never watch a video. Some topics, like a diagram in Biology or a practical in Chemistry, are easier to understand when shown. Use video when it adds something that text cannot, and use text for everything else.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Past questions and answers:</strong> small to download and the best form of exam practice.</li>
<li><strong>Short notes and summaries:</strong> read them offline once saved.</li>
<li><strong>Quiz apps with offline mode:</strong> good for practice, if the app lets you download content first.</li>
<li><strong>Textbooks and printed material:</strong> zero data, and often the best investment.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 4: Plan your downloads like a shopping list</h2>
<p>Before you connect, decide what you need for the next several days. This is the biggest habit change. Instead of opening your phone, connecting and browsing, you will connect with a list.</p>
[[flow Plan the week ; Make a download list ; Connect on Wi-Fi or a cheaper window ; Download everything ; Disconnect and study offline]]
<p>Here is how to build the list.</p>
[[steps Look at your timetable::Write down the subjects and topics you will study this week ; List the resources::For each topic, choose one note and one set of practice questions, plus a video only if needed ; Check sizes::If a file shows its size before you download, add it up so you stay within your budget ; Download in one session::Do it on free Wi-Fi at school, a library or a trusted place, or in one planned data session ; Test offline::Switch to airplane mode and confirm that each file opens]]
<p>Testing offline is important. Nothing is worse than reaching a topic and discovering that the file needs a connection to open. Do a quick check on the night you download.</p>

<h2>A worked example: planning one week on a small budget</h2>
<p>Here is an example with made-up numbers, to show how to think about a data budget. These are not real prices or file sizes. Use your own bundle and your own downloads.</p>
[[qa Suppose you have 600 MB for the week for study. A text pack with notes and past questions is 20 MB per subject for four subjects. One short video is 60 MB, and you want two videos. Do you fit in the budget, and how much is left?|Step 1: Text packs: 4 subjects x 20 MB = 80 MB ; Step 2: Videos: 2 x 60 MB = 120 MB ; Step 3: Total planned = 80 + 120 = 200 MB ; Step 4: Remaining = 600 - 200 = 400 MB|You fit easily and have 400 MB left. You could keep some in reserve for urgent downloads and use the rest for extra practice sets, but avoid spending it on streaming.]]
<p>The same method works with any numbers. Add up what you plan to download, compare it with your budget, and keep a small reserve. A weekly plan stops the mid-week panic.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Study offline, and make the offline time count</h2>
<p>Downloading is not learning. The real work happens when you disconnect and use the material. A simple routine for each study session works well.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Read or watch the topic once,</strong> and write three to five key points in your notebook.</li>
<li><strong>Close the material and recall.</strong> Write what you remember without looking.</li>
<li><strong>Answer past questions</strong> on that topic, and mark them.</li>
<li><strong>Log your mistakes</strong> in a small notebook with the correct answer and the reason.</li>
</ol>
<p>Study sessions of thirty to forty-five minutes, with a short break, usually work better than long sessions that drift. Put your phone in airplane mode during the session so notifications do not tempt you. This protects both your data and your focus.</p>
[[compare Data-wasting habit|Data-smart habit|Streaming every lesson live ; Leaving data on all day ; Browsing for notes each time you study ; Updating apps on mobile data|Downloading once and rewatching offline ; Switching data on only when needed ; Planning a weekly download list ; Updating apps on Wi-Fi]]

<h2>Make use of free Wi-Fi and shared access responsibly</h2>
<p>Schools, libraries, learning centres and some public places may offer Wi-Fi. If you can use it safely, use it for your big downloads. Be careful about security: do not log in to important accounts on public networks if you are not sure they are safe, and never share passwords.</p>
<p>You can also plan with friends or classmates. One person downloads a pack and shares it with others by a local transfer method on the phone, as long as the content is allowed to be shared. This is a common and sensible way to save data across a group. Check that you have the same, correct version of the material, and do not share anything that you are not permitted to share.</p>
<p>Some networks also have cheaper data at certain times of the day, or offers for particular bundles. These offers change often, so check your network's current options and use them when it suits your plan, rather than trusting old information from a friend.</p>

<h2>Low-data study when you have no data at all</h2>
<p>There will be weeks when you have almost nothing to spend. You can still make progress.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Redo what you already have:</strong> review notes, correct old mistakes and re-solve past questions you got wrong.</li>
<li><strong>Make your own flashcards on paper:</strong> a question on one side, an answer on the other. They cost almost nothing and are very effective.</li>
<li><strong>Study with a partner:</strong> ask each other questions from your notes.</li>
<li><strong>Teach a topic aloud:</strong> explaining something to a friend, or even to yourself, shows what you do not yet know.</li>
<li><strong>Borrow printed past questions or textbooks</strong> from a school, a library or a senior student.</li>
</ul>
<p>A student who has used a week well offline is not behind. The topics are the same. Only the delivery is different.</p>

<h2>What to look for in a study app or website</h2>
<p>If you use apps for practice, choose ones that respect your data. Before you commit to an app, check these points.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Does it let you download content?</strong> An app that needs a connection for every question will drain your data and fail you when the network drops.</li>
<li><strong>Is it light?</strong> Look at the download size before installing. A very large app takes a big bite of your bundle before you study anything.</li>
<li><strong>Does it load text first?</strong> Pages that load heavy images and adverts before showing the question waste data.</li>
<li><strong>Does it save your progress?</strong> You should not lose your results because a connection dropped halfway.</li>
<li><strong>Is the content reliable?</strong> Answers should be explained. A set of answers with no explanations teaches you little.</li>
</ul>
<p>For exam practice, a good routine is to download a set of past questions for a subject, study offline, and sync your progress when you are next connected. EliteScholars is built as a free exam-prep app for JAMB, WAEC, NECO and GST students, so use whichever tools suit your phone and your data, as long as they pass the checks above.</p>

<h2>Common myths about studying on limited data</h2>
<h3>Myth: "I cannot prepare well without video lessons."</h3>
<p>Videos can help explain a topic, but marks come from understanding and practice. Many students improve mostly by reading clear notes and answering past questions with corrections. Use video as a supplement.</p>
<h3>Myth: "More data means more learning."</h3>
<p>More data means more access, not more learning. Students with large bundles often spend them on entertainment and distraction. What matters is the time you spend actively working on questions.</p>
<h3>Myth: "Downloading everything is better."</h3>
<p>Downloading too much leaves you with a full phone and no plan. Download what you will use this week. You can always download more next week.</p>
<h3>Myth: "Airplane mode is only for flights."</h3>
<p>Airplane mode is one of the best study tools you have. It switches off the connection and the notifications in one tap and protects your data and your focus.</p>

<h2>Keeping your phone ready for study</h2>
<p>Your phone is your classroom, so keep it in good shape.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Free up storage:</strong> delete duplicate photos, forwarded videos and apps you do not use, so you have room for downloaded notes and questions. If you need to keep photos, move them to a safer place.</li>
<li><strong>Protect your battery:</strong> lower your screen brightness, switch on battery saver and charge when you can. Offline study uses less power than streaming.</li>
<li><strong>Back up your notes:</strong> keep a copy of your own notes and error log on paper or in another place, so one lost phone does not erase your work.</li>
<li><strong>Organise your files:</strong> create a folder for each subject and name files clearly, for example Biology-Genetics-notes. You will find them faster and waste less time.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Practising the exam format without data</h2>
<p>Many national exams are now taken on computer, so it helps to practise reading and answering on a screen. You can do that offline if your practice tool or app allows downloaded sets. Treat each practice set like the real exam: set a timer, do not look at your notes and do not check the answers until you finish.</p>
<p>After you finish, go through every wrong answer and write why it was wrong. This habit, more than the number of sets you attempt, is what lifts your marks. Ten sets done carefully beat thirty done quickly. If you can connect at the end of the week, you can sync your results and see your progress, but the learning has already happened offline.</p>
<p>Finally, build a small, honest budget. Decide how much you can spend on data each month, split it across weeks, and keep a note of what each download was for. When you see the plan on paper, the temptation to waste data on random browsing drops sharply.</p>

<h2>Sharing the load: study groups and data</h2>
<p>A small study group can cut your data costs and improve your learning at the same time. Instead of each person searching for the same material, divide the work. One person gathers notes for Biology, another for Chemistry, another for past questions. Then you share what you found, as long as the material is free to share, and test each other from it.</p>
<p>Meet in person where you can. A thirty-minute session where you each explain one topic aloud uses no data at all, and teaching a topic is one of the strongest ways to learn it. If you must meet online, use text messages or short voice notes rather than video calls, which use much more data.</p>
<p>Set simple rules for the group: start on time, keep phones on silent, and end with each person naming one thing they will practise before the next meeting. A group that gossips wastes time, but a group with a clear agenda saves you effort and money.</p>
<p>Lastly, review your data habits at the end of each month. Check which apps used the most data, how much of your bundle went to study and how much to other things, and whether your marks moved. Adjust the plan. Small adjustments made every month add up to a big saving over a year, and the learning you protect is worth far more than the data you save.</p>

<h2>A simple weekly routine</h2>
[[timeline Sunday::Plan the week and write the download list ; Monday::Download on Wi-Fi or in one data session, then test offline ; Tuesday to Friday::Study offline in short sessions, log mistakes ; Saturday::Take a timed practice set and review errors ; Sunday again::Check data use, adjust the plan]]
<p>If your data runs out early, do not panic. Move to the offline methods above. If you have data left over, use it for a well-chosen extra practice set rather than random browsing.</p>

<h2>What to do today</h2>
[[checklist Check which apps used the most data this week ; Turn on Data Saver and restrict background data ; Switch off auto-play and auto-download in your apps ; Write this week's topics and a download list ; Download notes and past questions in one session ; Test that each file opens in airplane mode ; Start your first offline study session of thirty minutes]]
`
